\section{Keeping the import cheap}

The package surface is not only a discoverability decision, it is also a
latency decision.  Host applications embed the CLI as a subcommand, so
[[import pytorial]] runs on every invocation of the host, whether or not
that invocation has anything to do with tutorials.  The CLI chapter
therefore loads its heavy dependencies at the point where they are used
rather than at module level (\cref{tutorial-cli}).

That kind of arrangement decays quietly.  A future contributor adds one
convenient module-level import, everything still works, and the cost
returns without a single failing test.  The tests below are what make the
decay loud: they assert not what the package \emph{does} but what it
declines to load.

<<test [[import_cost.py]]>>=
"""Tests that keep ``import pytorial`` cheap."""

import json
import os
import pathlib
import subprocess
import sys

import pytorial

<<import cost test functions>>
@

These tests cannot observe anything useful from inside the pytest process.
By the time a test runs, pytest has imported its own plugins, this test
module has imported [[pytorial]], and [[sys.modules]] holds a union of
everything anybody needed --- Rich among it.  The only place where the
question \enquote{what does this statement load?} has a meaningful answer
is an interpreter that has just started.

So each probe runs one statement in a fresh subprocess and reports the
module names that interpreter ended up with.  The snapshot is taken before
importing [[json]], so the reporting machinery cannot contaminate the
answer it is reporting.

We seed [[PYTHONPATH]] with the directory containing the package rather
than trusting the subprocess to inherit an import path.  The suite runs
against a working tree that may or may not be installed into the active
environment, and deriving the path from the already-imported
[[pytorial.__file__]] means the subprocess is guaranteed to import the same
copy the test process did.

<<import cost test functions>>=
def imported_module_names(statement):
    """Return the modules loaded by running `statement` in a new interpreter."""

    probe = (
        f"{statement}\n"
        "import sys\n"
        "loaded = sorted(sys.modules)\n"
        "import json\n"
        "print(json.dumps(loaded))\n"
    )
    package_root = pathlib.Path(pytorial.__file__).resolve().parent.parent
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["PYTHONPATH"] = os.pathsep.join(
        path for path in (str(package_root), env.get("PYTHONPATH")) if path
    )

    completed = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-c", probe],
        check=True,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        env=env,
    )
    return set(json.loads(completed.stdout))
@

The first probe is the headline claim: importing the package does not pull
in Rich.  We collect every offending module name and assert on the sorted
list rather than on a boolean, so a failure names the module that
reintroduced the dependency instead of merely reporting [[False]].

<<import cost test functions>>=
def test_importing_pytorial_does_not_import_rich():
    loaded = imported_module_names("import pytorial")

    offenders = sorted(
        name
        for name in loaded
        if name == "rich" or name.startswith("rich.")
    )
    assert offenders == []
@

Importing the package is the cheap case, though, and a lazy package surface
could satisfy the first test while still loading Rich the moment a host
touches anything.  The second probe closes that gap by exercising the call
a host actually makes: registering the tutorial subcommand.  Registration
builds the whole command tree, so if any command body reached Rich at
definition time this would catch it.

The probe passes no tutorial paths, which keeps it from reading the
catalog; it measures registration alone.  Alongside Rich we name
[[markdown_it]] and [[pygments]] explicitly, because those are the
transitive costs that make Rich expensive, and a partial import that
loaded them without loading [[rich]] itself would otherwise slip through.

<<import cost test functions>>=
def test_adding_a_typer_subcommand_does_not_import_rich():
    loaded = imported_module_names(
        "import typer, pytorial\n"
        "pytorial.add_typer_subcommand(typer.Typer())"
    )

    offenders = sorted(
        name
        for name in loaded
        if name.split(".")[0] in {"rich", "markdown_it", "pygments"}
    )
    assert offenders == []
@

The last probe guards the Markdown renderer on its own.  Rich imports its
Markdown support lazily too, so a change that loaded [[rich.console]] at
module level would fail the first test but might still leave the parser
untouched; asserting on the parser separately keeps the two costs
distinguishable when one of them regresses.

<<import cost test functions>>=
def test_importing_pytorial_does_not_import_the_markdown_renderer():
    loaded = imported_module_names("import pytorial")

    offenders = sorted(
        name
        for name in loaded
        if name.split(".")[0] in {"markdown_it", "mdurl", "pygments"}
    )
    assert offenders == []
@
